From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "David J. Vandenburgh" <david@vandenburgh.co>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] usb_gadget: let SuperSpeed bulk endpoints burst

The SuperSpeed endpoint companion descriptors were left all zero, so
bMaxBurst was 0: one 1024 byte packet per burst, each waiting for its
own handshake before the next may go. A SuperSpeed link moves most of
its data in bursts of up to 16 packets, and without them a copy runs at
a fraction of what the link can carry. The kernel's own mass storage
and network functions ask for the full 16.

Set bMaxBurst to 15 (16 packets) on the two bulk endpoints. The
interrupt endpoint keeps 0, and nothing changes at high or full speed,
which have no companion descriptor.
---
diff --git a/src/usb_gadget.c b/src/usb_gadget.c
index f44df9b..bb920a8 100644
--- a/src/usb_gadget.c
+++ b/src/usb_gadget.c
@@ -251,6 +251,12 @@ void fill_ep_descriptor(mtp_ctx * ctx, usb_gadget * usbctx,struct usb_endpoint_d
 
 		ss_descriptor->ep_desc_comp.bLength = sizeof(struct usb_ss_ep_comp_descriptor);
 		ss_descriptor->ep_desc_comp.bDescriptorType = USB_DT_SS_ENDPOINT_COMP;
+
+		// Let the host send or take up to 16 packets per burst on the bulk endpoints. With 0
+		// every 1024 byte packet is its own burst and waits for its own handshake, which
+		// keeps a SuperSpeed link far below what it can carry.
+		if(desc->bmAttributes == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_BULK)
+			ss_descriptor->ep_desc_comp.bMaxBurst = 15;
 	}
 #endif
 
